+## Rejected (with reason)
+- §4.9 'An observed move ... is tolerated and surfaced, not reverted ... reopening is promised only for closure' vs B11 and the 'only a plan can change its recorded placement' invariant — a moved-out node manager that is later closed is never reopened, and the observed move is a non-plan placement write. 2× REJECT, 1× CONFIRM Should-fix. The ground seat's code facts are concrete (src/observe/mutations.rs:571-573 sets moved_out with no path clearing it; src/reconcile/planner.rs:610 and :526 skip CreateTab/CreateWorkspace for a moved-out seat) but the refute seat verified the same facts and answers them: §4.9 names the existing moved_out handling, keeps it deliberately, logs a warning, and bounds the B11 promise to closure. This is the r1 fix for the prior "restores the recorded tab" finding choosing the tolerate-and-surface option the prior report offered. Default stands. Optional wording: B11's scope line could say "closed while in its recorded tab" and the invariant sentence could say "except an observed move".
+- §4.4 exit-5 row 'ClientError::Frame (I/O, timeout, truncated or undecodable frame) ... Retry in a bit'; hg-k4l.18 — a read timeout after a mutating request is an unknown outcome, and the retry text invites a blind duplicate (external: oneuptime.com timeout post, apsoai/cli#125). 3× REJECT. B2 ("its ok for cli when daemon is down to state to the agent to keep retrying in a bit") is the user's explicit wording; the text says "unavailable", not "did not happen"; mutating commands keep their command-specific arms before the fallthrough (§4.4 line 173) and plan apply has EXIT_ALREADY_APPLIED; the unknown-outcome risk predates this spec, which only relabels the stderr line. The cited sources are generic background. At most a one-clause hint ("if a mutating command timed out, check state before retrying").
+- §4.6 'A pointer to a seat that exists but is retired is transparent'; §4.7 'target seat exists and is not retired'; §4.11; §4.12 threads_seat 'else null' — an Inactive seat can be an effective manager with no agent to escalate to; no skip-to-active-ancestor rule and no guard on deactivating a seat with reports. 3× REJECT. B5 asks for an "arbitrary optional pointer" to a seat with "how they communicate is open"; §4.6 makes the seat, not the clone, the stable identity; the chain root (node manager) must be Active with a live clone (§4.9, B8, B11); §4.12 renders each chain seat's status and threads_seat null signals no live clone. Liveness-aware routing is a design extension the user did not request. Possible follow-up: a skill line telling a session to escalate to the node manager when its manager has no live session.
